WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
50% each leaves Brazil's presidency tied two days before Sunday's vote. This is Omni View Top World News, episode 32. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. After that race, the case on both sides of a New Jersey voter roll fight, then a sign of progress on a satellite link Ukraine uses in the war. Brazil put its last televised argument on the table Friday in Rio de Janeiro. Al Jazeera says President Luis Anacio Lula de Silva and Senator Flavio Bolsonaro questioned each other on the

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.620
economy, infrastructure and regional development. TV Globo carried the debate.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.860
The same report calls Friday more diplomatic than their faceoff on Sunday, and still full

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:44.560
of personal attacks. The New York Times identifies Flavio as the son of former president

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.880
Jair Bolsonaro and says the former president was jailed over a coup plot. That is background.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:56.180
It is not a call on Sunday. Bolsonaro finished the October 4th round with 47 percent. Lula

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:01.520
had 45. Datafola published its reading on Thursday. Two weeks earlier, published polls

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.540
had Bolsonaro ahead by four points. Al Jazeera's line is that the candidates are neck-and-neck.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.960
Both campaigns are expected in the streets of Rio and São Paulo before the runoff.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.140
Lula challenged a plan to take a chainsaw to public spending, the way he framed Argentina's

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.560
president, Javier Miele. Bolsonaro answered that he would have the same job as Miele, because,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.360
in his words, Lula is leaving the country bankrupt.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:30.940
He accused the incumbent of letting organized crime expand, driving families into debt and ignoring higher prices.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.320
Lula, called Brazil's first working-class president in that report, said Bolsonaro does not know what hunger is.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.340
Bolsonaro shot back. Why are things so expensive?

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.900
Lula said the senator does not know Brazil.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:50.020
Bolsonaro presented himself as the candidate of change, then came three tense exchanges, each acclaimed not a verdict.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.140
Bolsonaro called the 80-year-old president nine fingers and nicknamed tied to an accident

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.900
from Lula's years as a metal worker.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.760
Lula demanded an apology.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.320
Bolsonaro gave one.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.200
Lula called him a vigilante, pointing to allegations of ties to paramilitary groups

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:05.660
in Rio.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.420
Bolsonaro said he would classify those groups as narcoterrorists.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:13.760
He also faced the mother of a five-year-old killed in a shootout involving a policeman

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:14.780
who had been his client.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.000
Bolsonaro said he does not believe that client was guilty.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.580
The policeman is accused in that account only as someone involved, and as a client.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.380
He is not called guilty here.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:25.920
Sunday is the decision.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.900
The presidency is what changes, on the terms those two men argued, spending, crime, prices,

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.700
and who they say is fit to govern.

00:02:34.828 --> 00:02:38.808
New Jersey opened early voting Saturday with the case on both sides of who stays on the

00:02:38.808 --> 00:02:41.628
rolls, and New York opened early voting the same day.

00:02:42.068 --> 00:02:46.728
Democracy Docket says millions of voters in both states are walking into fights over eligibility,

00:02:46.728 --> 00:02:51.488
mail ballots and federal access to voter data. House Republicans subpoenaed

00:02:51.488 --> 00:02:55.548
acting Secretary of State Shirley Emma Halu on Wednesday for more documents on

00:02:55.548 --> 00:02:59.088
a Motor Vehicle Commission glitch and they say the state's response has been

00:02:59.088 --> 00:03:03.068
inadequate. That demand followed the state's refusal to hand over personally

00:03:03.068 --> 00:03:06.708
identifying information and a Justice Department lawsuit for broader

00:03:06.708 --> 00:03:12.948
registration data that New Jersey won in July. From June 2023 to June 2024, about

00:03:12.948 --> 00:03:17.988
6,600 people who answered that they were not United States citizens were marked as citizens

00:03:17.988 --> 00:03:19.288
and sent for registration.

00:03:19.848 --> 00:03:24.388
Governor Mickey Cheryl says fewer than 400 people newly registered by that error later

00:03:24.388 --> 00:03:24.808
voted.

00:03:25.248 --> 00:03:29.608
The state says those votes did not change any election, and that it has spent months removing

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:33.608
registrations that came only from the error, while checking people who may have another

00:03:33.608 --> 00:03:34.608
valid registration.

00:03:35.088 --> 00:03:40.068
In Ocean County, the elections board is reviewing dozens of Democratic and unaffiliated voters

00:03:40.068 --> 00:03:43.348
in bayhead, whose property tax bills go outside the burrow.

00:03:43.788 --> 00:03:48.048
Voting rights groups sued, saying the review discriminated against those voters and left

00:03:48.048 --> 00:03:50.068
similarly situated Republicans out.

00:03:50.568 --> 00:03:52.448
A judge declined last week to stop it.

00:03:52.848 --> 00:03:57.088
Their fear is a last-minute cleanup that makes eligible voters prove themselves at the polls,

00:03:57.428 --> 00:03:58.588
or after election day.

00:03:59.108 --> 00:04:03.428
Both states will also count male ballots cast on time that arrive after election day,

00:04:03.768 --> 00:04:08.168
and the report warns that unresolved congressional races could draw misinformation around that

00:04:08.168 --> 00:04:08.468
count.

00:04:08.468 --> 00:04:13.459
Ukraine has a sign of progress on the satellite links its forces use against Russian

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:14.199
attack drones.

00:04:14.699 --> 00:04:19.799
France 24 reports that Defense Minister Evheny Kamara says monitoring systems again show

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:24.819
Starlink and Starsheald above 400 kilometers per hour, and that the connection is stable.

00:04:25.339 --> 00:04:27.679
The ministry says the speed readings are back to normal.

00:04:28.159 --> 00:04:32.779
It does not say services fully restored, and the military does not publish a maximum speed.

00:04:33.219 --> 00:04:35.799
The slowdown was first reported on October 14.

00:04:35.799 --> 00:04:40.599
Ukrainian officials said it hurt interception of Russian attack drones and broke up front-line

00:04:40.599 --> 00:04:41.279
communications.

00:04:41.939 --> 00:04:46.119
Sources said speeds had fallen to about 160 kilometers per hour or less.

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:51.139
The New York Times calls Starsheald the Pentagon-funded network Ukraine uses to thwart those

00:04:51.139 --> 00:04:53.459
attacks after more than a week of disruption.

00:04:54.099 --> 00:04:57.679
Neither Elon Musk nor the United States government has taken responsibility.

00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:00.599
Each has said only the other could have made that call.

00:05:01.219 --> 00:05:05.359
Reuters has reported that the order to throttle Starsheald came from inside the White House.

00:05:05.359 --> 00:05:10.039
President Donald Trump said Friday that if the access problems continued, he would get

00:05:10.039 --> 00:05:10.799
it straightened out.

00:05:11.179 --> 00:05:13.379
That's OmniView top-world news for today.

